---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../../components/DocsSidebar.astro";

// Numbers measured 2026-10-29 by scripts/benchmark-runtime.mjs and the
// manual install/bundle audit recorded in ROADMAP.md (R13, phase 1).
const runtime = [
  { sprites: "100", fps: "60.0", avg: "16.66", p95: "17.7", heap: "17.3", growth: "−1.0" },
  { sprites: "1,000", fps: "60.0", avg: "16.64", p95: "17.8", heap: "21.5", growth: "+2.1" },
  { sprites: "5,000", fps: "59.9", avg: "16.71", p95: "18.0", heap: "44.3", growth: "+10.7" },
  { sprites: "20,000", fps: "23.8", avg: "41.69", p95: "57.8", heap: "75.4", growth: "−4.7" },
  { sprites: "50,000", fps: "9.7", avg: "102.96", p95: "128.9", heap: "169.4", growth: "−1.1" },
];

const bundle = [
  { name: "g2w — hello-world demo build (own code + pixi.js + effect)", size: "683 KB / 209 KB gzip" },
  { name: "g2w — own packages only", size: "113 KB / ~37 KB gzip" },
  { name: "Kaplay", size: "738 KB" },
  { name: "MelonJS", size: "2.6 MB" },
  { name: "Excalibur", size: "7.4 MB" },
  { name: "PixiJS", size: "17.9 MB" },
  { name: "Three.js", size: "19 MB" },
  { name: "Babylon.js (core)", size: "23.5 MB" },
  { name: "Phaser", size: "46 MB / ~8.9 MB gzip" },
];

const install = [
  { name: "Kaplay", time: "1.1 s", size: "3.8 MB" },
  { name: "Excalibur", time: "1.9 s", size: "8.5 MB" },
  { name: "Three.js", time: "6.7 s", size: "19.5 MB" },
  { name: "PixiJS", time: "8.3 s", size: "80.1 MB" },
  { name: "g2w minimal (core + renderer-pixi + input-dom)", time: "~17 s", size: "80.3 MB" },
  { name: "g2w full set (core + runtime + renderer-pixi + input-dom)", time: "13.4 s", size: "131.4 MB" },
  { name: "Phaser", time: "13.5 s", size: "107.4 MB" },
  { name: "MelonJS", time: "14.2 s", size: "5.1 MB" },
  { name: "Babylon.js", time: "22.9 s", size: "69.4 MB" },
];
---

<Base title="Benchmarks · g2w docs">
  <div class="docs">
    <DocsSidebar active="benchmarks" />

    <main class="docs-main prose">
      <div class="crumb">docs / benchmarks</div>
      <h1>Benchmarks</h1>
      <p>
        Every performance claim on this site traces back to a runnable script.
        This page publishes the first recorded R13 measurements — how the
        runtime scales, what an install and a shipped bundle weigh, and the
        honest caveats attached to each number.
      </p>

      <h2 id="method">Methodology</h2>
      <p>
        The runtime harness (<code>scripts/benchmark-runtime.mjs</code>) boots
        the hello-world Vite dev server, opens a dedicated benchmark page in
        headless Chrome over the Chrome DevTools Protocol, and samples a fixed
        10-second window per run — three runs per case, each with a fresh page
        navigation, summarized as the per-metric median. The page builds N
        orbiting sprites through the public API only — the core
        <code>Game</code> loop, <code>GameObjectCollection</code>, and
        <code>Sprite2D</code> + <code>createSpriteView</code> — with an 8×8
        texture generated locally, so the measurement covers the sprite
        pipeline, not downloads. Frame intervals come from a ring buffer
        inside the update hook; heap numbers come from
        <code>performance.memory</code>. Set <code>G2W_BENCH_RUNS</code> to
        change the run count.
      </p>
      <div class="callout" style="box-shadow: none">
        <span class="tag">Environment</span>
        <span>
          Windows 11 x64, HeadlessChrome 156, median of 3 runs per case,
          sampled 2026-10-29. Machine- and browser-dependent: treat the values
          as order-of-magnitude evidence, re-run on your own hardware with
          <code>node scripts/benchmark-runtime.mjs</code>. The harness is
          browser-dependent and deliberately <b>not</b> part of
          <code>pnpm test</code> or CI.
        </span>
      </div>

      <h2 id="runtime">Runtime scaling</h2>
      <div class="pkg-table" style="box-shadow: none">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Sprites</th><th>FPS</th><th>Avg frame (ms)</th><th>p95 frame (ms)</th><th>Heap (MB)</th><th>Heap growth (MB)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {runtime.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="name">{row.sprites}</td>
                <td>{row.fps}</td>
                <td>{row.avg}</td>
                <td>{row.p95}</td>
                <td>{row.heap}</td>
                <td>{row.growth}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Up to 5,000 independently animated sprites the loop rides the
        requestAnimationFrame cap (60 fps, ~16.7 ms average). The saturation
        point sits between 5,000 and 20,000 sprites, where the per-sprite
        <code>view.sync()</code> projection — no batching, no texture atlas —
        becomes the dominant cost. Heap stays in a 17–44 MB band up to 5,000
        sprites and scales with the workload beyond it (75–170 MB at 20k/50k).
      </p>

      <h2 id="bundle">Bundle size</h2>
      <p>
        Raw size of the JavaScript each npm tarball would ship to the browser
        (gzip where measured). g2w's own packages are small; what a player
        downloads is dominated by the renderer you pick.
      </p>
      <div class="pkg-table" style="box-shadow: none">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Package</th><th>Shipped JS (raw / gzip)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {bundle.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="name">{row.name}</td>
                <td>{row.size}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="install">Install footprint</h2>
      <p>
        Developer-side cost: one warm-cache <code>npm install</code> per
        package and the resulting <code>node_modules</code> size. The g2w
        packages themselves are ~0.3 MB — the disk weight is the dependencies
        you pull in. The <b>minimal set</b> (core + renderer + DOM input,
        booting the core <code>Game</code>) installs pixi.js only, which makes
        it about the same size as PixiJS itself; adding
        <code>@g2w/runtime</code> for the Effect-native <code>G2WGame</code>
        lifecycle brings effect's ~51 MB on disk
        (<a href="/docs/packages#footprint">see the footprint breakdown</a>).
      </p>
      <div class="pkg-table" style="box-shadow: none">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Package</th><th>Install time</th><th>node_modules</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {install.map((row) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="name">{row.name}</td>
                <td>{row.time}</td>
                <td>{row.size}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 id="caveats">What these numbers do not say</h2>
      <div class="callout" style="box-shadow: none">
        <span class="tag">Caveats</span>
        <span>
          <b>One machine, headless browser.</b> The median-of-3 aggregation
          removes single-run noise (the single-run preview was markedly more
          pessimistic at high sprite counts), but the runtime table still
          describes g2w only — no competitor runs the same sprite workload
          yet, so no cross-framework FPS claim is made here; the bundle and
          install tables are static measurements, not behavior. A shared
          sample game per framework is the remaining R13 acceptance criterion.
        </span>
      </div>

      <h2 id="next">Next steps</h2>
      <p>
        Re-run the harness yourself with
        <code>node scripts/benchmark-runtime.mjs</code> (configure via
        <code>G2W_BENCH_CASES</code> and <code>G2W_BENCH_SECONDS</code>), or
        read the <a href="/docs/roadmap">roadmap entry R13</a> for what comes
        next.
      </p>
    </main>

    <aside class="docs-toc">
      <div class="ttl">On this page</div>
      <a class="on" href="#">Benchmarks</a>
      <a href="#method">Methodology</a>
      <a href="#runtime">Runtime scaling</a>
      <a href="#bundle">Bundle size</a>
      <a href="#install">Install footprint</a>
      <a href="#caveats">What these numbers do not say</a>
      <a href="#next">Next steps</a>
    </aside>
  </div>
</Base>
